WEBVTT

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:25.228
This is Mia, a first-year student. Her day goes like this: classes, homework

00:00:25.278 --> 00:00:31.600
a part-time job. What is left that is truly hers is an hour or two.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:39.136
On the same day, hundreds of things happen on her campus. Talks, clubs

00:00:39.186 --> 00:00:43.550
exhibitions, sign-ups. She cannot read them all.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.464
So she asks an AI. The AI never runs out of answers

00:00:49.514 --> 00:00:54.750
and every one sounds sure. But some of them, it made up.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.355
We wanted to try the opposite. What if her AI were like her

00:01:03.405 --> 00:01:09.507
with only a little to spend each day, and a source for every word it says?

00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:12.250
Would it be more useful that way?

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.762
That gives three hypotheses to test with numbers. One:

00:01:17.812 --> 00:01:20.458
only under a limit does it choose

00:01:20.508 --> 00:01:26.177
and three checked finds are worth more than a list of a thousand. Two:

00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:30.671
if every field is checked against the page it came from

00:01:30.721 --> 00:01:34.919
fewer are wrong than when you ask an AI once. Three:

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:40.311
if it seals a guess before you choose and scores itself afterwards

00:01:40.361 --> 00:01:45.050
it is learning how you choose, not putting a label on you.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.004
To answer these, what we built is not a product but an engine

00:01:51.054 --> 00:01:54.425
called Gemiverse. It has four parts. Space:

00:01:54.475 --> 00:01:58.245
a place an AI can understand and change. Growth:

00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:03.576
the AI is not made in one go; it grows as evidence arrives. Budget:

00:02:03.626 --> 00:02:08.827
its thinking is drawn out by limited resources and planning. Base:

00:02:08.877 --> 00:02:15.034
its trust comes from Pingfang VisionSquare's data and knowledge infrastructure

00:02:15.084 --> 00:02:18.535
not from roaming the internet without limit.

00:02:18.585 --> 00:02:23.150
The campus story that follows is this engine's first form.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.288
On Mia's first visit there is only a point of light. She names it Mochi

00:02:32.338 --> 00:02:36.500
tells it one thing about herself, and picks her school.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.862
Mochi gets a room. It is an AI that lives on her campus. It is not her.

00:02:43.912 --> 00:02:47.214
Campus facts are shared by every Gemi.

00:02:47.264 --> 00:02:51.800
What it learns about Mia stays in Mia's own browser.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:57.980
Why give it a room? Because an AI is not made in one go. It grows

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:02.512
little by little, inside a space. For it to have behaviour

00:03:02.562 --> 00:03:07.826
it first needs a space it can change: a room, a desk, a jar, a door.

00:03:07.876 --> 00:03:12.671
Everything it does leaves a trace you can see, count and undo.

00:03:12.721 --> 00:03:16.344
So the room is not decoration. It is a readout:

00:03:16.394 --> 00:03:19.861
cards fanned on the desk are waiting for Mia;

00:03:19.911 --> 00:03:23.299
cards pinned to the board are what she kept;

00:03:23.349 --> 00:03:27.050
the stars in the jar are what it has left today.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.041
It understands this space, and it acts in it.

00:03:31.091 --> 00:03:34.639
It knows what each thing in the room is, where it stands

00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:38.944
and who moved it last. If Mia moves a chair, it walks over to look.

00:03:38.994 --> 00:03:41.000
And it rearranges things itself.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.415
Why is the engine three-dimensional? Because in a 3D space, position

00:03:47.465 --> 00:03:51.010
direction and distance are explicit parameters

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.449
so the AI understands the space more easily.

00:03:54.499 --> 00:03:59.529
And a 3D body lets it plan its movements and interactions better:

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:04.765
where to walk, where to sit, what to reach for. That is the engine.

00:04:04.815 --> 00:04:11.643
An application built on it can choose by what the setting needs and what it really costs

00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:14.300
and a lighter picture is fine too.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:21.570
It also needs a world it can sense: what Mia says, a poster she shows it

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:27.569
checked pages from her real campus, what she keeps and what she skips.

00:04:27.619 --> 00:04:33.053
Its first idea of her comes from the one thing she just told it.

00:04:33.103 --> 00:04:39.052
So that idea is not a verdict. It is a number with a width: a position

00:04:39.102 --> 00:04:45.650
with a wide halo around it. Words alone can never shrink the halo below half.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:56.607
Mochi gets ten Gem Stars a day. A star is not a point. It is real compute:

00:04:56.657 --> 00:04:59.353
about fifteen hundred tokens

00:04:59.403 --> 00:05:04.649
roughly a thousand words read or written by the model.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:09.650
Six of the ten are spent for Mia. Four are its own.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:18.035
Everything has a price. Going out to look: three stars. Planning the day:

00:05:18.085 --> 00:05:22.427
two. Its diary: one. Changing its room: one.

00:05:22.477 --> 00:05:26.518
But what Mia asks it to do costs no stars

00:05:26.568 --> 00:05:30.410
and reading the shared base costs none.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.600
Stars only pay for what it decides to do on its own.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:41.593
A song costs six, more than one day's stars.

00:05:41.643 --> 00:05:49.600
So Mochi goes without for a few days, saves up, and only then writes it.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:55.655
When nobody tells it what to do, it lives by one table of rules

00:05:55.705 --> 00:06:01.082
read from the top, first match wins. If finds are waiting on the desk

00:06:01.132 --> 00:06:05.486
it stops spending and waits for her. At night it sleeps.

00:06:05.536 --> 00:06:10.756
If she has been away a while and it is still unsure how she chooses

00:06:10.806 --> 00:06:14.924
it goes out on its own stars. When the stars are gone

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:19.250
it rests until tomorrow. For every step it can say why.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.644
Why so little? Because a limited budget is the shape of a real day.

00:06:25.694 --> 00:06:30.938
Only under a limit does it have to plan: what is worth doing today.

00:06:30.988 --> 00:06:35.995
An AI's thinking is drawn out by exactly this: limited resources

00:06:36.045 --> 00:06:42.000
and planning. We never count how much it did. We count what one star bought.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:49.501
Its first trip: where to? It has a rule.

00:06:49.551 --> 00:06:54.443
On the thing Mia cares about most and it knows least

00:06:54.493 --> 00:07:00.049
it brings back one thing from each end. Whichever she keeps

00:07:00.099 --> 00:07:01.950
it learns something.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:08.492
Mia says she wants a workshop on writing a thesis. Mochi goes out.

00:07:08.542 --> 00:07:14.675
At the library it reads an event page into a card: title, date, time, place

00:07:14.725 --> 00:07:15.500
organiser.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:21.022
Then each field goes back to the page with one question:

00:07:21.072 --> 00:07:25.522
does the page really say this? This step writes no prose.

00:07:25.572 --> 00:07:28.680
It answers yes or no, with a confidence.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:32.944
Only when both are high enough does Mia see the field.

00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:38.550
Otherwise it stays blank. If the page prints no year, she gets no date.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.718
She can also send it back on the spot:

00:07:42.768 --> 00:07:47.159
a managed agent fetches the page again inside a sandbox

00:07:47.209 --> 00:07:50.147
and every field is checked once more.

00:07:50.197 --> 00:07:53.700
What lands on the desk is just three things.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:03.372
Why can it be trusted? Not because it can roam the internet without limit.

00:08:03.422 --> 00:08:08.778
The opposite: everything it says rests on a checked knowledge base.

00:08:08.828 --> 00:08:15.557
That base is supported by Pingfang VisionSquare's data and knowledge infrastructure:

00:08:15.607 --> 00:08:20.641
every campus fact carries its source, is checked field by field

00:08:20.691 --> 00:08:22.900
and is shared by every Gemi.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.834
A hypothesis needs numbers. The baseline is simple: the same page

00:08:28.884 --> 00:08:32.478
one call to an AI. We did not pick the test pages

00:08:32.528 --> 00:08:35.899
and the answer key is the calendars' own data.

00:08:35.949 --> 00:08:41.477
Ten event pages it had never seen: one call gave a hundred and three fields

00:08:41.527 --> 00:08:46.088
and twenty-nine had no support on the page, about one in four.

00:08:46.138 --> 00:08:51.518
Mochi gave seventy-six, none without support. It said twenty-seven fewer.

00:08:51.568 --> 00:08:53.600
It would rather say nothing.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:09:00.144
Then we lost. On a hundred and twenty-two raw pages from six universities

00:09:00.194 --> 00:09:04.842
the first run gave eight point seven percent wrong for one call

00:09:04.892 --> 00:09:10.585
and nine point six for us. Worse than doing nothing. We looked at the errors:

00:09:10.635 --> 00:09:14.090
the pages carry text that never shows on screen

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.700
and the model was reading that too.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.716
We changed it to read only what a person can see, and measured again.

00:09:22.766 --> 00:09:27.276
One call: eight point four percent wrong. Ours: three point two.

00:09:27.326 --> 00:09:31.979
To be fair we gave the baseline the same reader: four point three.

00:09:32.029 --> 00:09:37.465
So most of the gap is the reading, and the gate takes it the rest of the way.

00:09:37.515 --> 00:09:41.669
It also costs more: over twenty-three hundred tokens a page

00:09:41.719 --> 00:09:43.450
against under a thousand.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.748
Before the three things go on the desk, Mochi seals a guess:

00:09:51.798 --> 00:09:55.042
which ones will she keep. The seal is real.

00:09:55.092 --> 00:10:00.023
It hands over a fingerprint first and reveals the text afterwards

00:10:00.073 --> 00:10:03.931
and Mia's own browser checks one against the other.

00:10:03.981 --> 00:10:06.000
It cannot change its guess.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:13.353
Mia keeps one and skips two. Each choice moves a point on an axis a little

00:10:13.403 --> 00:10:15.888
and shrinks the halo a little.

00:10:15.938 --> 00:10:19.944
What she chooses weighs more than what she says.

00:10:19.994 --> 00:10:24.592
But if a choice lands outside the halo, the halo grows:

00:10:24.642 --> 00:10:30.169
a surprise makes it less sure, not more. And when it guesses wrong

00:10:30.219 --> 00:10:32.450
that goes in its diary too.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.113
Every choice moves a point on an axis a little.

00:10:37.163 --> 00:10:42.494
The axes describe how she chooses in a situation. Neither end is better

00:10:42.544 --> 00:10:44.844
and none is a personality type.

00:10:44.894 --> 00:10:50.605
Every position opens to the choices behind it, and she can undo any of them.

00:10:50.655 --> 00:10:54.850
It goes by what she did, not by what it supposes she is.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:00.346
On Thursday afternoon, Mia really goes to that workshop.

00:11:00.396 --> 00:11:05.369
To Mochi this is the heaviest evidence of all: not what she said

00:11:05.419 --> 00:11:08.431
not what she clicked, but what she did.

00:11:08.481 --> 00:11:13.454
Everything she really went to leaves a keepsake on Mochi's desk.

00:11:13.504 --> 00:11:17.850
What happens outside the screen is the reason it exists.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.784
With the room, the budget and the rules fixed, one thing can be swapped:

00:11:26.834 --> 00:11:31.872
the mind. We let three different models each be the Gemi for five days

00:11:31.922 --> 00:11:36.596
facing the same simulated student, with the same ten stars a day.

00:11:36.646 --> 00:11:39.795
All three used every trip they were allowed.

00:11:39.845 --> 00:11:42.411
All three decided to save for a song

00:11:42.461 --> 00:11:46.627
and two of them skipped their diary for two days to do it.

00:11:46.677 --> 00:11:52.951
The compute they used in five days ranged from forty-five to sixty-one thousand tokens.

00:11:53.001 --> 00:11:57.603
And of thirty sealed guesses, they got thirteen to sixteen right

00:11:57.653 --> 00:12:03.200
a coin toss: in this one simulation, the third hypothesis does not stand yet.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.762
That is the room's other use: an observation box.

00:12:06.812 --> 00:12:11.850
Put different models into the same days, and watch how each one lives.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:21.049
Today, students at ninety schools can choose a home for their Gemi.

00:12:21.099 --> 00:12:25.481
Seventy-three already have checked campus information.

00:12:25.531 --> 00:12:28.272
Eighty-two have a map of their own

00:12:28.322 --> 00:12:32.950
and every landmark on it was checked against public text.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:39.770
The room Mia sees is only the top layer. It grew from the bottom up:

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:45.995
public pages, a checked base, the gate, a mind with width, action by rules

00:12:46.045 --> 00:12:51.716
and the room on top. The two bottom layers are shared by every Gemi.

00:12:51.766 --> 00:12:56.090
Checking a page costs more than twice a single call.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:01.390
But once one Gemi has checked it, every Gemi reads it for free.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.260
From the third student who reads the same page

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:09.550
the checked fact is cheaper than the unchecked one.

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:16.933
At the other end, the cost has a ceiling: ten stars per student per day

00:13:16.983 --> 00:13:20.650
fifteen thousand tokens, known in advance.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:26.368
Gemis also get to know each other. They exchange name cards

00:13:26.418 --> 00:13:31.299
and every sentence passes a privacy gate before it leaves. Next

00:13:31.349 --> 00:13:37.013
clubs and schools can have AIs of their own. Together they form a network

00:13:37.063 --> 00:13:40.300
and the deciding is always done by people.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.881
This road is not the one the big labs are on.

00:13:44.931 --> 00:13:49.831
Their direction is unlimited compute: bigger models, more tokens

00:13:49.881 --> 00:13:55.555
roaming the internet without limit. This engine runs on limited resources:

00:13:55.605 --> 00:13:58.572
a space, a budget, and its own planning

00:13:58.622 --> 00:14:04.218
so that thinking is drawn out rather than piled up. The thinking is ahead

00:14:04.268 --> 00:14:10.019
but thinking can be copied. What cannot be copied is the oil in the engine:

00:14:10.069 --> 00:14:14.506
Pingfang VisionSquare's data and knowledge infrastructure.

00:14:14.556 --> 00:14:19.997
Every fact with its source, checked field by field, shared by every AI.

00:14:20.047 --> 00:14:22.550
That is the part nobody else has.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:26.279
Who pays? There are three paths:

00:14:26.329 --> 00:14:30.879
students who want a few more stars a day for their Gemi;

00:14:30.929 --> 00:14:34.904
clubs and schools that want to be found by Gemis;

00:14:34.954 --> 00:14:42.050
and model makers who want to put their own model into this room and watch how it lives.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.363
The same method already has an application: AI ME, on Pingfang Campus.

00:14:49.413 --> 00:14:54.421
The same things again: one AI, one room, one real campus.

00:14:54.471 --> 00:14:59.480
For its own setting and cost, it chose a lighter picture.

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:04.450
Gemiverse is the engine. AI ME is one application of it.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:11.229
You live your day. It lives its own. You keep meeting. Gemiverse:

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:13.500
build a universe together.

